## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** Countable Choice, a bounded Lipschitz domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb R^n$, and $u\in BV(\Omega)$.

[F1] Every smaller Lipschitz graph chart has a linear one-sided $L^1$ trace, its local norm estimate, and the local BV Green formula. Traces agree on overlapping charts. The same result supplies the endpoint case in dimension one. ([[lem-ip-bv-one-sided-trace-on-lipschitz-graph]])

[F2] Normalized Hausdorff measure on a Lipschitz graph has density $\sqrt{1+|Dh|^2}$, with its almost-everywhere unit outward normal. ([[lem-ip-lipschitz-graph-area-and-calculus-under-countable-choice]])

[F3] Compact sets admit finite smooth cutoff covers, and finite signed Radon measures are determined by smooth compactly supported tests. ([[lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls]], [[lem-ip-distributional-radon-representation-unique]])

[F4] A finite vector of signed Radon measures has finite Radon partition variation. ([[lem-ip-vector-radon-variation-is-radon]])

## Proof

**Given:** Countable Choice and the bounded Lipschitz domain in the Statement.

1.1 If $\Omega=\varnothing$, its BV space and boundary $L^1$ space are both the zero space, the zero trace is unique, and every formula and estimate holds with both sides zero. Assume henceforth $\Omega\ne\varnothing$. Shrink the finitely many boundary graph neighborhoods to smaller charts still covering the compact boundary and with positive margins inside the original charts. Each smaller graph base is bounded and its Lipschitz constant finite, so [F2] gives finite surface measure. In each chart [F1] defines a local trace and bounds its surface $L^1$ norm, using [F2], by a chart constant times $\|u\|_1+|Du|(\Omega)$. On overlaps these local traces agree by [F1]. Consequently they define one class $Tu\in L^1(\partial\Omega,\mathcal H^{n-1})$ with $\|Tu\|_1\le C(\|u\|_1+TV(u;\Omega))$. Linearity follows locally from [F1], hence globally. [F1, F2, given, construct]

2.1 Construct a finite smooth partition equal to one on a neighborhood of $\overline\Omega$, subordinate to the boundary charts and the interior open set $\Omega$: cover this compact set by finitely many balls with doubled closures in those open sets, take the bumps in [F3], and divide them by their positive sum near $\overline\Omega$. Multiply by one further smooth cutoff supported where the sum is positive and equal to one near $\overline\Omega$. Apply the local Green identity [F1] to each chart-supported field $\eta_j\phi$. For the interior-supported field the defining distributional identity applies. Sum the identities. Since $\sum_j\eta_j=1$ and $\sum_j\nabla\eta_j=0$ near $\overline\Omega$, the interior and boundary sums are exactly $$\int_\Omega u\operatorname{div}\phi=-\int_\Omega\phi\cdot dDu+\int_{\partial\Omega}Tu\,\phi\cdot\nu\,d\mathcal H^{n-1}.$$ The argument first applies to smooth fields. Compactly supported $C^1$ fields and their first derivatives are uniformly approximated by mollification, which passes every term by finite variation and $L^1$ integrability. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, construct, algebra]

3.1 Extend each coordinate of $Du$ by zero from $\Omega$ to $\mathbb R^n$. It is a finite signed Radon measure: compact inner approximation can be performed inside $\Omega$, and outer approximation in the ambient space follows by applying that inner approximation to the complement and using finiteness. The boundary term $Tu\,\nu\mathcal H^{n-1}|_{\partial\Omega}$ is also finite signed Radon coordinatewise, by [F2], compactness and the $L^1$ bound in step 1.1. Thus their difference $\mu=i_\#Du-Tu\,\nu\mathcal H^{n-1}|_{\partial\Omega}$ is a finite Radon vector measure by [F4]. The formula in step 2.1 says exactly that $D\widetilde u=\mu$, so $\widetilde u\in BV(\mathbb R^n)$. [F2, F4, step 1.1, step 2.1, construct]

4.1 The two summands defining $\mu$ in step 3.1 are supported on the disjoint Borel sets $\Omega$ and $\partial\Omega$. The partition definition of variation therefore gives the sum of their variations. For a unit measurable vector density $\nu$ and scalar density $Tu$, the boundary variation is $|Tu|\mathcal H^{n-1}$: the triangle inequality gives the upper bound, and a finite partition of the unit sphere into pieces of diameter below $\epsilon$, applied to the direction $\operatorname{sgn}(Tu)\nu$, gives the lower bound $(1-\epsilon)|Tu|\mathcal H^{n-1}$ by projection; let $\epsilon\downarrow0$. Hence $TV(\widetilde u)=TV(u;\Omega)+\|Tu\|_1$. Step 1.1 gives the asserted bound. If another trace satisfied the Green identity, their difference times $\nu\mathcal H^{n-1}$ would integrate to zero on all smooth tests, hence be zero by [F3]; the same variation calculation gives equality of the trace classes. [F3, step 1.1, step 3.1, algebra]

5.1 In dimension one, each smaller graph neighborhood contains one boundary point, so the finite graph cover makes the boundary finite. A bounded open set with finite boundary is a finite disjoint union of bounded intervals. Use the one-sided endpoint construction in [F1] on each of their finitely many endpoints. The partition argument of step 2.1 and the disjoint interior/boundary measure calculation in steps 3.1–4.1 apply verbatim with $\mathcal H^0$ counting measure and outward signs $-1$ at a left endpoint and $+1$ at a right endpoint. This proves the stated dimension-one companion. [F1, F3, step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, construct] ∎ 